{"records":[{"id":"vfpxcbk5bx","title":"Dataset of a Retrospective Multicenter Cohort Study on Characteristics of Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor-induced Encephalitis and Comparison with HSV-1 and Anti-LGI1 Encephalitis ","description":"Until now, data on clinical features, diagnostic criteria and treatment of irEncephalitis have been limited to case reports and series with small sample sizes. Data to describe clinical differentiators from other types of encephalitis is largely missing. However, patients with extensive malignant disease are also at risk of infectious encephalitis, such as herpes simplex virus (HSV)-1 encephalitis. The objective of this dataset is to provide data on clinical, laboratory, and radiologic features of irencephalitis and allow for comparison with herpes simplex virus (hsv)-1 encephalitis and anti-leucine-rich glioma-inactivated (anti-lgi)-1 encephalitis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-25T14:41:39.287Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vfpxcbk5bx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vfpxcbk5bx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Leonie","last_name":"Müller-Jensen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sarah ","last_name":"Zierold","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Judith","last_name":"Versluis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wolfgang","last_name":"Boehmerle","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Petra","last_name":"Huehnchen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthias","last_name":"Endres","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raphael","last_name":"Mohr","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Annette","last_name":"Compter","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian U ","last_name":"Blank","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tim","last_name":"Hagenacker","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Friedegund","last_name":"Meier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"L","last_name":"R","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anja","last_name":"Gesierich","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Salzmann","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Hassel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Selma","last_name":"Ugurel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lisa","last_name":"Zimmer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patricia ","last_name":"Banks","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lavinia","last_name":"Spain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jennifer A","last_name":"Soon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tomohiro","last_name":"Enokida","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Makoto","last_name":"Tahara","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katharina C","last_name":"Kähler","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ruth","last_name":"Seggewiss-Bernhardt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Catriona","last_name":"Harvey","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Georgina V","last_name":"Long","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Florian","last_name":"Schöberl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Louisa","last_name":"von Baumgarten","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Hundsberger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Max","last_name":"Schlaak","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lars","last_name":"French","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Samuel","last_name":"Knauss","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lucie","last_name":"Heinzerling","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Clinical Neurology","Neurotoxicity","Immunotherapy","Clinical Oncology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"h4rtjx4w2h","title":"Data for comparative analysis on the physicochemical properties of tick-borne encephalitis virus envelope protein residues that affect its antigenic properties","description":"deteminant.txt - file with antigenic determinants of protein E of tick-borne encephalitis virus in fsata format\nR_script.txt - script in programming language P for statistical data processing\nsubtyp_tabl.txt - the correspondence table of the sequence number from the database to the subtypes of the virus\nTBE_E_protein.txt - file with Amino acid sequences of E protein of tick-borne encephalitis virus from the Genbank database in fasta format\nunequal_deteminant.txt - file with amino acid sequences unequal antigenic determinants of E protein of tick-borne encephalitis viru in fasta format\nunequal_subtyp_tabl.txt - the correspondence table amino acid sequences unequal antigenic determinants to the subtypes of tick-borne encephalitis virus\namin.csv - table of physico-chemical properties of amino acid residues","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-07-03T01:50:52.739Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h4rtjx4w2h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h4rtjx4w2h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yurij","last_name":"Bukin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sergey","last_name":"Tkachev","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Irina","last_name":"Kozlova","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexey","last_name":"Paramonov","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vladimir","last_name":"Zlobin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Ruzek","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhangyi","last_name":"Qu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yurij","last_name":"Dzhioev","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Array","Protein Properties","Statistical Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x4483f3rmk","title":"Dataset for 'LRP8 is an entry receptor for tick-borne encephalitis viruses'","description":"This dataset contains the raw data supporting all main and supplementary figures used in the manuscript \"LRP8 is an entry receptor for tick-borne encephalitis viruses\" published in PNAS. In this study, we expressed 13 different family members on the surface of cells and demonstrated that LRP8 promotes infection of tick-borne encephalitis viruses (TBEV). Validation studies confirmed direct binding of TBEV and LRP8, and a key role for LRP8 in neuronal cell infection. Our findings also enabled the generation of an inhibitory LRP8-Fc decoy molecule that blocks TBEV infection. ","source":{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-16T19:59:15.375Z","url":"https://digitalcommonsdata.wustl.edu/datasets/x4483f3rmk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x4483f3rmk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pengfei","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sean","last_name":"Hui","orcid":"0000-0002-9870-6823"},{"first_name":"Zhenlu","last_name":"Chong","orcid":"0000-0001-6195-3452"},{"first_name":"Olivier","last_name":"Escaffre","orcid":"0000-0003-0402-9698"},{"first_name":"Michael N.","last_name":"Nguyen","orcid":"0000-0002-4184-7078"},{"first_name":"Stefanie P.","last_name":"Muraro","orcid":"0000-0002-5105-6659"},{"first_name":"Hana","last_name":"Janova","orcid":"0000-0003-2578-0982"},{"first_name":"Hongming","last_name":"Ma","orcid":"0000-0001-5105-1332"},{"first_name":"Shiqi","last_name":"Cao","orcid":"0009-0006-9371-1216"},{"first_name":"Tomasz","last_name":"Kaszuba","orcid":"0000-0002-5967-4670"},{"first_name":"Brian","last_name":"Imbiakha","orcid":"0000-0001-8538-5445"},{"first_name":"Sathvik","last_name":"Palakurty","orcid":"0000-0002-2222-4553"},{"first_name":"Madison L.","last_name":"Pearson","orcid":"0009-0005-9931-9269"},{"first_name":"David A.","last_name":"Price","orcid":"0000-0002-0751-9943"},{"first_name":"Gaya K.","last_name":"Amarasinghe","orcid":"0000-0002-0418-9707"},{"first_name":"Daisy W.","last_name":"Leung","orcid":"0000-0002-7189-9557"},{"first_name":"Shannan L.","last_name":"Rossi","orcid":"0000-0003-2450-2374"},{"first_name":"Alexander N.","last_name":"Freiberg","orcid":"0000-0001-8413-8960"},{"first_name":"Daved H.","last_name":"Fremont","orcid":"0000-0002-8544-2689"},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Diamond","orcid":"0000-0002-8791-3165"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"U01AI073755"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"U19AI181960"}],"categories":["Microbiology","Virology"],"journal_issn":["10916490"]},{"id":"yvkrb4pmcb","title":"Data for: Immune protection by potent neutralizing anti-Japanese encephalitis virus monoclonal antibodies derived from vaccination","description":"The is the processed data containing body temperature and viral loads of serum and tissues collected from animals that were passively immunized with neutralizing monoclonal antibodies and intradermally challenged with Japanese encephalitis virus.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-26T21:02:55.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yvkrb4pmcb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yvkrb4pmcb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yan-Jang","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dana","last_name":"Vanlandingham","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amy","last_name":"Lyons","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephen","last_name":"Higgs","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ashley","last_name":"Doerfler","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Greg","last_name":"Peterson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ashley","last_name":"Zelenka","orcid":null},{"first_name":"So Lee","last_name":"Park","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Konner","last_name":"Cool","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei-Wen","last_name":"Hsu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Victoria","last_name":"Ayers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Susan","last_name":"Hettenbach","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Antibody"],"journal_issn":["01663542"]},{"id":"823tckxwtm","title":"Basic patches on the E2 glycoprotein of eastern equine encephalitis virus influence viral vascular clearance and dissemination","description":"Abstract: Previously we found SINV-EEEV particles can be removed from the murine blood circulation in a phagocyte-dependent manner which can be disrupted by either transient depletion of vascular heparan sulfate glycosaminoglycans (GAG) or mutation of the viral E2 glycoprotein (K71/74/77A) associated with decreased GAG binding in vitro. Here, we further investigate the viral determinants of EEEV vascular clearance and evaluate their role in viremia development. We identified two large basic patches on the EEEV E2 glycoprotein which contain two known GAG-binding sites (K71/74/77 and K156/R157) and six additional basic residues (K10, R13, K56, R152, K231, and K232). We find that disruption of either basic patch by single alanine substitutions promotes prolonged retention of SINV-EEEV particles in the murine blood circulation in an experimental viremia model. Furthermore, like the attenuated K71/74/77A mutant, we observed the K156/R157A, K10A, and K231A mutations are also associated with similar enhanced viral dissemination in a mouse infection model as the attenuated K71/74/77A mutant. Surprisingly, despite known differences in GAG-binding and potential alteration in receptor interactions, we find the initial dispersal of WT and mutant SINV-EEEV virions from the inoculation site to the draining lymph node to be equivalent at 1 hour post-subcutaneous inoculation. Moreover, our data suggests the higher viremia associated with mutation of the E2 basic patches may be attributed to evasion of viremic control by blood-filtering phagocytes. Overall, this study defines viral features of the EEEV E2 glycoprotein that influence tissue-specific viral dissemination and highlights the capacity of blood-filtering phagocytes to modulate EEEV viremia.\n\nData description: Datasets are qPCR results quantifying viral genomes in each sample. For each sample, RNA was extracted and used to generated cDNA; the cDNA was analyzed by TaqMan qPCR against a standard curve for quantification.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-12T19:51:45.047Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/823tckxwtm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/823tckxwtm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tem","last_name":"Morrison","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Arbovirus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hzzbg2z7bm","title":"Japanese encephalitis virus infection increases blood-brain barrier permeability in a human model by inducing a strong pro-inflammatory endothelial response","description":"Japanese encephalitis (JE) continues to be the most important cause of viral encephalitis globally. Following the bite of an infected mosquito, Japanese encephalitis virus (JEV) replicates in the skin and local lymph nodes before crossing the blood-brain barrier (BBB - the physiological barrier that protects the brain from blood-borne toxins and pathogens) to enter the brain. Little is known about the exact mechanisms of JEV entry across the BBB into the brain. Data from clinical studies suggest the BBB is disrupted in JE, leading to brain swelling, and that a strong pro-inflammatory cytokine response may be important in this. To better understand the disease mechanisms, we established a BBB model using human brain cells. We demonstrated that JEV infection of the BBB triggers a host inflammatory response, which impairs virus production but causes disruption of the BBB, thus allowing viral entry into the brain. Our data showed that the corticosteroid drug dexamethasone, which is sometimes used to treat JE in Asia, reduces the inflammatory response and the BBB disruption. This data set supporting the studies described in the related article provides new insights into the complex interactions between the BBB and JEV, and the potential role of anti- inflammatory treatment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-04-09T02:51:48.41Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hzzbg2z7bm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hzzbg2z7bm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Adjanie","last_name":"Patabendige","orcid":null},{"first_name":" Benedict","last_name":"Michael","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alister ","last_name":"Craig","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tom","last_name":"Solomon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Blood-Brain Barrier","Corticosteroid","Cytokine Response","Viral Encephalitis","In Vitro Study"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3thfbs4y72","title":"The vitamin D receptor is essential for the replication of pseudorabies virus","description":"Dear ASM Journals Ethics, \nFirstly, we would like to thank you for your valuable contribution to the seamless publication of our article. Secondly, we apologize for the issues with the original data that we submitted. We have addressed your questions individually. Please review and let us know if there are any further concerns. We will do our utmost to assist you in completing the review process, ensuring that the article progresses smoothly to the next stage.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-19T17:47:43.732Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3thfbs4y72","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3thfbs4y72"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"shengli","last_name":"ming","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Original Order"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t2phwr39xf","title":"Waheed et al., 2023 PNAS Data","description":"HIV-1 assembly occurs at the inner leaflet of the plasma membrane in highly ordered membrane microdomains. The size and stability of membrane microdomains is regulated by activity of the sphingomyelin hydrolase neutral sphingomyelinase 2 (nSMase2) that is localized primarily to the inner leaflet of the plasma membrane. In this study, we demonstrate that pharmacological inhibition or depletion of nSMase2 in HIV-1-producer cells results in a block in the processing of the major viral structural polyprotein Gag and the production of morphologically aberrant, immature HIV-1 particles with severely impaired infectivity. We find that disruption of nSMase2 also severely inhibits the maturation and infectivity of other primate lentiviruses HIV-2 and simian immunodeficiency virus, has a modest or no effect on non-primate lentiviruses equine infectious anemia virus and feline immunodeficiency virus, and no effect on the gammaretrovirus murine leukemia virus. These studies demonstrate a key role for nSMase2 in HIV-1 particle morphogenesis and maturation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-08T06:50:27.253Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t2phwr39xf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t2phwr39xf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Abdul","last_name":"Waheed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanan","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eva ","last_name":"Agostino","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lwar ","last_name":"Naing","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuta ","last_name":"Hikichi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ferri ","last_name":"Soheilian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Seung-Wan ","last_name":"Yoo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yun ","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peijun ","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Barbara ","last_name":"Slusher ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Norman ","last_name":"Haughey","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eric","last_name":"Freed","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"67gnvw3g3r","title":"Virus-Receptor Interactions of Glycosylated SARS-CoV-2 Spike and Human ACE2 Receptor.Zhao et al","description":"Additional supplemental data for the article \"Virus-Receptor Interactions of Glycosylated SARS-CoV-2 Spike and Human ACE2 Receptor\" by Zhao et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-01T20:49:11.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/67gnvw3g3r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/67gnvw3g3r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Peng","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Molecular Mechanics with Molecular Dynamics","3D Analysis","Glycomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mhfrh9whrd","title":"Phagocyte-expressed glycosaminoglycans promote capture of alphaviruses from the blood circulation in a host species-specific manner","description":"Abstract\nThe magnitude and duration of vertebrate viremia are critical determinants of arbovirus transmission, geographic spread, and disease severity—yet, mechanisms determining arbovirus viremia levels are poorly defined. Previous studies have drawn associations between in vitro virion-glycosaminoglycan (GAG) interactions and in vivo clearance kinetics of virions from blood circulation. From these observations, it is commonly hypothesized that GAG-binding virions are rapidly removed from circulation due to ubiquitous expression of GAGs by vascular endothelial cells, thereby limiting viremia. Using an in vivo model for viremia, we compared the vascular clearance of low and enhanced GAG-binding viral variants of chikungunya, eastern- (EEEV), and Venezuelan- (VEEV) equine encephalitis viruses. We find GAG-binding virions are more quickly removed from circulation than their non-GAG-binding variant; however individual clearance kinetics vary between GAG-binding viruses, from swift (VEEV) to slow removal from circulation (EEEV). Remarkably, we find phagocytes are required for efficient vascular clearance of some enhanced GAG-binding virions. Moreover, transient depletion of vascular heparan sulfate impedes vascular clearance of only some GAG-binding viral variants and in a phagocyte-dependent manner, implying phagocytes can mediate vascular GAG-virion interactions. Finally, in direct contrast to mice, we find enhanced GAG-binding EEEV is resistant to vascular clearance in avian hosts, suggesting the existence of species-specificity in virion-GAG interactions. In summary, these data support a role for GAG-mediated clearance of some viral particles from the blood circulation, illuminate the potential of blood-contacting phagocytes as a site for GAG-virion binding, and suggest a role for species-specific GAG structures in arbovirus ecology.\n\nData consists of reverse-transcriptase quantitative PCR data to measure viral genome quantities in samples.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-13T10:24:45.561Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mhfrh9whrd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mhfrh9whrd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tem","last_name":"Morrison","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Arbovirus"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122052,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":97,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122052,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121585,"restricted":58,"embargoed":409},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1908},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":608},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":430},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":388},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":370},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":347},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":310},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":217},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":206}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":119516},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1873},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":163},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":120},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":68},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":51},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":34},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":28},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":402},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":356},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":352},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":323},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":273}]}}